A rest slot may be scoped, and its bare children read the parameters the
declaration names.

That is the one place a slot's parameter name is the whole binding rather than
just contract. A named population writes its own names at its own binding
site; children written bare have no site to write them at, so the names in
`content ...component(n int) ui.node` are what the caller reads. Renaming one
is a breaking change to every caller, the same as for a named slot.

It was refused outright until `lib/ui/window.sngl` needed it -- a window hands
its route parameters to the body it renders through exactly this. A second,
unscoped rest slot beside it is not the way out: a component declares at most
one, so bare children would have nowhere unambiguous to land.

The splice is what this pins. `ir.SlotBody` returns the declaration's
parameters for a rest slot the way it returns a population's for a named one,
so both inliners bind the insertion's arguments without either growing a case.
Left out, the children were spliced with the parameter unbound and every read
of it rendered as nothing at all, silently, on every target.

deny out/none/html/index.html `n is <` -- the parameter is bound, not spliced away to nothing
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `"n is "\)` -- same, on the target that renders through fmt.Sprint

-- scoped.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component row(content ...component(n int) ui.node) ui.node {
    ui.vbox {
        content(7)
    }
}

ui.window #home(title="t") {
    row {
        component content(n) {
            ui.text(value="n is {n}")
        }
    }
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
